  "worked_solution": "First, we draw the corresponding straight line $y=x-1.$ Since the inequality involves a 'strictly greater than' sign, the line $y=x-1$ itself is *not* part of the solution. Therefore, we draw a **dashed line.** \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/3e3db53eb3112defdccbabf730f3e3e1cbecfa331b3855149079093a5485e4a9.png)\n\nThen, we must decide whether to shade above or below the line. The inequality $y>x-1$ refers to the $y$-values that are *greater than* those on the line $y=x-1,$ so we shade *above* the line.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/da907a9c86d1e152abf397afb9d733cf8dbbc924f581a5c8974c5a33353b86e7.png)\n\nLet's now check that we've shaded the correct part. First, we consider a point in the shaded region, say $(0,2).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14274623/assets/442425d9e00b4b83d592d33008728dfaf4f7543d626dc29aaf2a1ed395073511.png)\n\nWhen we substitute the coordinates of this point into our inequality, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & >x-1 \\\\ (2) & >(0)-1 \\\\ 2 & >-1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis indicates that we have shaded the correct region.",
